---
title: Partitioning
order: 1
description: Disk partitioning and device identification
tags: [disk, partition, fdisk, gdisk, parted, lsblk, blkid]
---

Tools for creating, modifying, and inspecting disk partition tables. Use gdisk for GPT disks (UEFI systems), fdisk for MBR or GPT, and parted for scripted operations.

@text
List block devices and identify disks before partitioning.

@code bash
# List all block devices with size, type, and mountpoints
lsblk

# Detailed output with filesystem type and UUIDs
lsblk -f

# Show only disks (no partitions)
lsblk -d

# Show specific columns
lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,TYPE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINT,UUID

# Identify block device attributes (UUID, TYPE, LABEL)
blkid

# Query a specific device
blkid /dev/sda1

# Show only UUID
blkid -s UUID -o value /dev/sda1

@text
fdisk — interactive MBR/GPT partition editor.

@code bash
# List partition tables on all disks
sudo fdisk -l

# List partitions on a specific disk
sudo fdisk -l /dev/sda

# Open interactive partition editor
sudo fdisk /dev/sda
# Key commands inside fdisk:
#   p    print partition table
#   n    new partition
#   d    delete partition
#   t    change partition type
#   w    write changes and exit
#   q    quit without saving

# Non-interactive: dump partition table as sfdisk script
sudo sfdisk -d /dev/sda > partitions.bak

# Restore partition table from backup
sudo sfdisk /dev/sda < partitions.bak

@text
gdisk — GPT-aware partition editor (preferred for UEFI systems).

@code bash
# List GPT partitions
sudo gdisk -l /dev/sda

# Open interactive GPT editor
sudo gdisk /dev/sda
# Key commands inside gdisk:
#   p    print partition table
#   n    new partition
#   d    delete partition
#   t    change type code (EF00=EFI, 8300=Linux, 8200=swap)
#   i    info on a partition
#   w    write and exit
#   q    quit without saving

# Convert MBR to GPT (non-destructive but back up first)
sudo sgdisk -g /dev/sda

# Backup GPT table
sudo sgdisk -b gpt-backup.bin /dev/sda

# Restore GPT table
sudo sgdisk -l gpt-backup.bin /dev/sda

@text
parted — scriptable partition editor supporting MBR and GPT.

@code bash
# List all partitions
sudo parted -l

# Interactive mode
sudo parted /dev/sda

# Create a GPT label
sudo parted /dev/sda mklabel gpt

# Create a 512 MiB EFI partition starting at 1 MiB
sudo parted /dev/sda mkpart primary fat32 1MiB 513MiB
sudo parted /dev/sda set 1 esp on

# Create a Linux partition using remaining space
sudo parted /dev/sda mkpart primary ext4 513MiB 100%

# Print partition table
sudo parted /dev/sda print

# Resize a partition (parted only moves the end boundary; resize filesystem separately)
sudo parted /dev/sda resizepart 2 100%

# Non-interactive scripted partitioning
sudo parted -s /dev/sda \
  mklabel gpt \
  mkpart primary fat32 1MiB 513MiB \
  set 1 esp on \
  mkpart primary ext4 513MiB 100%

@text
Inform the kernel of partition changes without rebooting.

@code bash
# Re-read partition table (older method)
sudo partprobe /dev/sda

# Or use blockdev
sudo blockdev --rereadpt /dev/sda
